From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Roman Leventov <leventov.ru@gmail.com>
Date: Tue, 4 Aug 2026 17:39:00 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 21/24] runtime: harden backend memory pressure

Harden the composed memory-control, bounded-context, and local-Node lifecycle patches at their integration boundaries. Resolve the process's effective cgroup-v2 directory through procfs mount metadata instead of assuming the hierarchy root. Validate memory and Node settings before database initialization, reject unsupported non-Linux controls, and pass every documented setting through the stock self-hosted Compose file.

Run duration-unbounded allocator trim on a detached native worker so asynchronous runtime shutdown cannot join it. Keep one-second cgroup and external-shedding samples active while one trim runs, defer new owner reclamation only while headroom remains above the shedding boundary, and resample after completion. Validate bounded malloc_info output structurally and report unsupported allocators before sampling.

Track memory-pressure publications independently of health and RSS checks so a clear and re-entry always starts a new continuous-pressure grace period, including when watch updates coalesce. Prioritize a ready pressure publication over a simultaneously completed health check and scope health latency to the health RPC rather than joined RSS sampling.

On a representative long-running self-hosted workload, one glibc trim took 4.122 seconds and reduced cgroup use by about 6.35 GiB. The result demonstrates both the value and the multi-second latency of explicit trimming, motivating detached execution and continued pressure sampling rather than treating trim as a short blocking operation.
